# Integrality over K[j] transports along K-algebra maps

Theorem statement: `AlgebraicCurve.isIntegral_adjoin_map_algHom`
(`576940b4-b9e7-522d-8843-0ef8f323df09`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `0db3c6f8-d12a-5e11-8eff-c69b60e1465b`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$, $F$ and $F'$ be commutative rings with $F$ and $F'$ both $K$-algebras, and let $\varphi
\colon F \to F'$ be a homomorphism of $K$-algebras. Let $j, x \in F$, and suppose that $x$ is
integral over the $K$-subalgebra $\mathrm{Adjoin}_K\{j\} \subseteq F$ generated by $j$, that is, $x$
satisfies a monic polynomial with coefficients in that subalgebra. The conclusion is that
$\varphi(x) \in F'$ is integral over the $K$-subalgebra $\mathrm{Adjoin}_K\{\varphi j\} \subseteq
F'$ generated by $\varphi(j)$. Integrality here is `IsIntegral` of an element of $F$ (respectively
$F'$) over the corresponding subalgebra regarded as a base ring, the ring structure on the target
being the one induced by the ambient algebra.

This is the routine transport of the relation 'integral over $K[j]$' along a $K$-algebra
homomorphism; in the formalisation it moves integrality over $\mathbb{Q}[j]$ from one coefficient
ring of formal/Laurent series to another, for instance along a coefficient embedding
$\mathbb{Q}((q)) \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$. It is used in the treatment of modular units,
cuspidality and modular forms mod $p$, where elements are recognised as integral over the $j$-line
after a change of coefficients.

